# Azure Identity and Access Management (IAM)

## 1. The Real-World Analogy

Think of an office building with three areas: **Lobby**, **Cafeteria**, and a **Secure Data Centre**.

| Person | Valid ID? | Lobby | Cafeteria | Data Centre |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Outsider / Hacker | ❌ No | ❌ Blocked at entrance | ❌ | ❌ |
| DevOps Engineer | ✅ Yes | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Allowed | ❌ Not authorised |
| Network Engineer | ✅ Yes | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Allowed | ✅ Authorised |

Two distinct concepts are at play:
- **Authentication** — verifying that the person has a valid ID to enter the building.
- **Authorisation** — deciding which areas that person is permitted to access once inside.

---

## 2. Authentication vs Authorisation

| Concept | Question it answers | Azure implementation |
|---|---|---|
| **Authentication (AuthN)** | *Who are you?* — Is this a valid identity? | Creating a user / service principal in Microsoft Entra ID |
| **Authorisation (AuthZ)** | *What can you do?* — What actions are permitted? | Assigning roles via RBAC |

**Why this matters in a real org:**

Giving every person in the organisation admin access is dangerous — a single mistake (accidental deletion, misconfiguration) can affect everything. IAM ensures:
- Every person is authenticated (given a user identity).
- Each person is only authorised to do what their role requires.

---

## 3. Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD)

**Microsoft Entra ID** is the Azure service that manages identity and access. It was previously called:
- **Active Directory (AD)**
- **Azure Active Directory (AAD)**

It is now called **Microsoft Entra ID**.

**Navigation:** Azure Portal → search **Microsoft Entra ID**

Key sections in the left menu:

| Section | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Users** | Create and manage individual user identities |
| **Groups** | Organise users into logical collections |
| **Roles and Administrators** | View and assign built-in roles; create custom roles (requires Entra ID Premium P1/P2) |
| **App Registrations** | Register applications that need access to Azure resources |

---

## 4. Users and Groups

### Creating a User

1. Microsoft Entra ID → **Users** → **New User** → **Create new user**.
2. Fill in:
- **User principal name** (e.g., `adar@mydomain.onmicrosoft.com`)
- **Display name** (e.g., `Adar`)
- **Password** — auto-generate and copy it (the user must change it on first login).
3. Click **Review + Create** → **Create**.

> A newly created user has **no permissions** by default. They are authenticated (can sign in) but not authorised to do anything. Attempting to create a Resource Group or VM will fail with a permissions error.

### Groups

As an organisation grows, managing roles for individual users becomes unscalable.

**Problem:** 100 developers each have a developer role. A new role needs to be added to all of them — updating 100 users individually is error-prone and time-consuming.

**Solution:** Create a group (e.g., `Developers`), add all developer users to it, and assign the role to the **group**. Adding or removing a role from the group instantly applies to all members.

```
Without groups:
  User1 → assign role   User2 → assign role   ... User100 → assign role

With groups:
  Group: Developers → assign role
    ├── User1
    ├── User2
    └── ... User100
```

---

## 5. Roles and RBAC

**RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)** is the authorisation system in Azure. Roles define what actions an identity (user, group, or resource) is allowed to perform.

### Built-in roles (examples)

| Role | Permissions |
|---|---|
| **Owner** | Full access — create, read, update, delete, and manage access |
| **Contributor** | Create, read, update, delete resources — but cannot manage access |
| **Reader** | Read-only access — view resources but cannot modify anything |
| **Storage Blob Data Owner** | Full access to blob storage containers and data |
| **Storage Blob Data Reader** | Read-only access to blob storage data |

### Assigning a role to a user

1. Microsoft Entra ID → **Users** → select the user.
2. Click **Assigned Roles** → **Add assignments**.
3. Select the appropriate role → **Add**.

> Custom roles (tailored to specific organisational needs) require **Microsoft Entra ID Premium P1 or P2**.

---

## 6. Identities — Users vs Resources

IAM in Azure covers two distinct types of identities:

| Identity Type | Examples | Authorisation mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| **Human identities** | Developers, QA engineers, managers, DevOps engineers | Users → Groups → Roles (RBAC) |
| **Resource identities** | Virtual machines, AKS pods, CI/CD pipelines, Terraform | Service Principal / Managed Identity |

**Common resource-to-resource scenarios:**

- A VM needs to read a file from Blob Storage.
- An AKS pod needs to access Azure File Storage.
- A CI/CD pipeline (Azure DevOps) needs to write artefacts to Blob Storage.
- Terraform needs to store its state file in Blob Storage.

For all of these, the Azure resource needs its own **identity** so it can be granted a role — just like a human user would be.

---

## 7. Service Principal vs Managed Identity

Both are used to give Azure resources an identity for authenticating to other Azure services. Underneath, Azure creates a **Service Principal** in both cases.

| Feature | Service Principal | Managed Identity |
|---|---|---|
| Created by | Developer / DevOps engineer manually | Azure automatically |
| Credential rotation | Must be manually managed (rotate keys on schedule) | Azure rotates automatically |
| Security maintenance | Developer's responsibility | Azure's responsibility |
| Best for | Third-party tools or cross-tenant access where Managed Identity isn't supported | Azure-to-Azure resource communication |
| Recommended? | Only when Managed Identity is not an option | ✅ Preferred for most Azure use cases |

### Two types of Managed Identity

| Type | Description |
|---|---|
| **System-assigned** | Created and tied to a single specific resource. Deleted automatically when the resource is deleted. |
| **User-assigned** | Created independently and can be assigned to multiple resources. |

> For most use cases, **system-assigned managed identity** is the simpler and recommended starting point.

---

## 8. Hands-On Demo — VM Accessing Blob Storage via Managed Identity

### Architecture

```
[Virtual Machine]
      │ (system-assigned managed identity)
      │ assigned role: Storage Blob Data Owner
      ▼
[Storage Account]
  └── [Container: test-container]
          └── index.html  ← VM reads this file via shell script
```

### Step 1 — Create a Resource Group

```bash
# Portal: search "Resource Groups" → Create
Name: managed-identity-poc
Region: East US
```

### Step 2 — Create a Storage Account and Upload a File

1. Search **Storage Accounts** → **Create**.
- Name: `managedpocstorage` *(must be globally unique)*
- Resource Group: `managed-identity-poc`
- Region: `East US`
2. Go to the storage account → **Containers** → **+ Container**.
- Name: `test-container`
3. Open `test-container` → **Upload** → select any file (e.g., `index.html`).

### Step 3 — Create a Virtual Machine

1. Search **Virtual Machines** → **Create** → **Azure Virtual Machine**.
2. Key settings:
- Name: `managed-demo-vm`
- Region: `East US` *(must match the storage account region)*
- Image: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (Free Services Eligible)
- Authentication: Password — `azureuser` / `<your-password>`
3. Click **Review + Create** → **Create**.

### Step 4 — Enable System-Assigned Managed Identity on the VM

1. Go to the VM → scroll down to **Identity** in the left menu.
2. Under **System assigned** tab, toggle **Status** to **On**.
3. Click **Save**.

> Azure automatically creates a Service Principal and links it to this VM.

### Step 5 — Assign a Role to the Managed Identity

1. Go to the **Storage Account** → **Access Control (IAM)**.
2. Click **Add** → **Add role assignment**.
3. Select role: **Storage Blob Data Owner**.
4. Click **Next** → **Assign access to: Managed identity**.
5. Click **Select members** → filter by **Virtual machine** → select `managed-demo-vm`.
6. Click **Review + assign**.

**Verify:** Go back to the VM → **Identity** → **Azure role assignments** — the `Storage Blob Data Owner` role on the storage account should be listed.

### Step 6 — SSH into the VM

```bash
ssh azureuser@<VM-PUBLIC-IP>
```

### Step 7 — Install jq (JSON parser)

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install jq -y
```

### Step 8 — Fetch the Managed Identity Access Token

```bash
ACCESS_TOKEN=$(curl -s \
  "http://169.254.169.254/metadata/identity/oauth2/token?api-version=2018-02-01&resource=https://storage.azure.com/" \
  -H "Metadata: true" | jq -r '.access_token')

echo $ACCESS_TOKEN
```

> The URL `169.254.169.254` is the Azure Instance Metadata Service endpoint — available only from within an Azure VM. It returns credentials for the managed identity assigned to the VM.

### Step 9 — Read the File from Blob Storage

```bash
STORAGE_ACCOUNT="managedpocstorage"
CONTAINER_NAME="test-container"
BLOB_NAME="index.html"

curl -s \
  "https://${STORAGE_ACCOUNT}.blob.core.windows.net/${CONTAINER_NAME}/${BLOB_NAME}" \
  -H "x-ms-version: 2020-04-08" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${ACCESS_TOKEN}"
```

**Expected output:** The contents of `index.html` printed in the terminal — confirming that the VM successfully accessed the Blob Storage using its managed identity without any hardcoded credentials.

### Step 10 — Cleanup

```bash
# In the portal: Resource Groups → managed-identity-poc → Delete resource group
```

---

## 9. Summary

| Concept | Description |
|---|---|
| **Authentication** | Verifying identity — *who are you?* |
| **Authorisation** | Granting permissions — *what can you do?* |
| **Microsoft Entra ID** | Azure's IAM service (formerly Azure AD / AAD) |
| **User** | Individual human identity in Entra ID |
| **Group** | Collection of users; assign roles at group level for easier management |
| **Role (RBAC)** | Defines what actions an identity can perform on Azure resources |
| **Service Principal** | Application/resource identity managed manually; requires key rotation |
| **Managed Identity** | Azure-managed identity for resources; automatic credential rotation |
| **System-assigned MI** | Tied to one resource; auto-deleted when the resource is deleted |
| **User-assigned MI** | Created independently; can be shared across multiple resources |

### IAM Decision Flow

```
Is the identity a human?
  YES → Create User in Entra ID → Add to Group → Assign Role
  NO  → Is it an Azure resource (VM, AKS, pipeline)?
          YES → Enable Managed Identity → Assign Role on target resource
          NO  → Use Service Principal (e.g., third-party tool, cross-tenant)
```
